INTENTIONAL DEVIATION FROM UPSTREAM: architect decision D5 (PR #4).

Formerly the oracle case testdata/upstream/python_line_breaks; the inputs are
unchanged. A form feed (\f) appears inside a line, and inside the function
line that git copies into the hunk section text.

Upstream splits lines with Python's str.splitlines, which also breaks at \f,
\v, \x1c-\x1e, \x85, U+2028, U+2029 and a lone \r. Every later number in the
numbered views then drifts from the file's real line numbers, an upstream
bug. review-mcp splits at "\n" only, so numbered line numbers equal the real
"\n"-based line numbers, which the DQ-12 snippets and links (P4) and the v2
anchoring depend on. Per the lead decision on the D5 implementation, "\r\n"
is one line ending and is dropped as before, so CRLF files render unchanged;
a lone "\r" is ordinary content, as in git.

How the outputs were derived: by the upstream oracle (gen_goldens.py) on a
placeholder twin, not by hand. The twin has these inputs with every \f
replaced by U+E000. U+E000 is neither a Python line break nor whitespace, and
it does not occur otherwise, so upstream treats it as an ordinary character,
just as review-mcp treats \f. Every U+E000 in the twin's out.* files was then
replaced back by \f. The \f in the base line "\f" is whitespace to Python's
strip, but the pre-context check strips both sides of the comparison, so the
substitution does not change any comparison. See testdata/upstream/README.md,
section "Deviations".
